{
    "background_story": "Hector was a 5th-century monk who lived in the Eastern Roman Empire. Born into a devout Christian family, he became fascinated with the teachings of Nestorius, the Patriarch of Constantinople. Hector was particularly drawn to Nestorius' emphasis on the distinction between the divine and human natures of Christ. As the controversy surrounding Nestorianism grew, Hector found himself at the forefront of the debate. He traveled extensively, engaging in theological discussions and defending the views of his mentor. Despite the Council of Ephesus' condemnation of Nestorianism, Hector remained committed to the cause, earning him both admiration and criticism from his peers. His writings on the subject would later influence the development of Eastern Christian theology, cementing his place in the annals of ecclesiastical history.",
